namespace pdg { /**
\fn pdg::Sprite::addAnimationDrawable(Drawing drawing, object options)

\brief attach an editable Drawing to an animation bone

Use this overload to attach artwork, such as a weapon or a marker, that follows
a bone as the sprite animates. Create the artwork with
\ref pdg::createDrawing() "pdg.createDrawing()" and the ordinary Drawing methods,
such as \ref Drawing::addRect() "Drawing.addRect()" or
\ref Drawing::addImage() "Drawing.addImage()".
The drawing uses coordinates relative to the selected bone; PDG positions,
rotates, and scales it with the bone's final animation pose.

\param drawing The Drawing to attach. Its contents remain editable after
registration, including through ElementRef objects returned by its methods.
\param options An object selecting the bone, placement, and optional drawing
settings. See \ref animation_drawable_options "Drawing options" below.

\return A nonzero unsigned integer identifying the registration. Pass this ID to
setAnimationDrawableEnabled(), removeAnimationDrawable(), or
getAnimationDrawableError().

\note Call enableAnimationPose() successfully before registering artwork. The
sprite's animations must have a fixed hierarchy: their active bones and bindings,
and their parent relationships, must remain consistent across clips.

\par Example: attach a marker to a hand
This example assumes \c sprite is a Spriter sprite in a visible layer, with a
\c reference animation and a bone named \c hand. Use the clip and bone names from your
own artwork.

\code{.js}
if (!sprite.enableAnimationPose('reference')) {
    throw new Error(sprite.getAnimationRigError());
}

const artwork = pdg.createDrawing();
const marker = artwork.addRect(
    new pdg.Rect(-3, -3, 3, 3),
    new pdg.Attributes().fillColor('yellow')
);

const drawableId = sprite.addAnimationDrawable(artwork, {
    bone: 'hand',
    placement: pdg.animationDraw_AfterAll
});

// A later edit appears on the next draw; no new registration is needed.
marker.setAttributes(new pdg.Attributes().fillColor('cyan'));

// When the marker is no longer needed:
// sprite.removeAnimationDrawable(drawableId);
\endcode

\htmlonly[block]
<div class="shared-api-options">
\endhtmlonly

\anchor animation_drawable_options
\par Drawing options (both overloads)
Both overloads accept the following \c options fields. Only \c bone is always
required. Use the numeric \c pdg constants for placement and stroke space;
strings such as \c "afterAll" or \c "local" are not accepted.

| Field | Type | Default | Meaning |
| ----- | ---- | ------- | ------- |
| \c bone | string or integer | Required | Bone name or zero-based bone ID. See getAnimationBoneNames(). |
| \c placement | integer | \c pdg.animationDraw_AfterAll | Where to draw relative to the sprite's authored images; see the placement table below. |
| \c slot | string | Empty string | Image binding name. Required for slot placements; omit it for \c pdg.animationDraw_BeforeAll and \c pdg.animationDraw_AfterAll. See getAnimationBindingNames(). |
| \c order | integer | \c 0 | Lower values draw first within the same placement and slot. Equal values preserve registration order. |
| \c strokeSpace | integer | \c pdg.animationStroke_PortPixels | How line thickness responds to scale; see Stroke thickness below. |
| \c bounds | object | Omitted | Rectangle with finite numeric \c left, \c top, \c right, and \c bottom fields in bone coordinates. Must include all artwork and stroke extents. |
| \c uncullable | boolean | \c true without bounds; \c false with bounds | When true, prevents the artwork from being skipped based on its declared bounds. |

\par Placement
A slot is a named image binding in the authored animation. It is separate from
the bone that positions your drawing. Slot placements require \c options.slot
to identify an existing image binding.

| Constant | Value | Placement |
| -------- | ----- | --------- |
| \c pdg.animationDraw_BeforeAll | 0 | Before all authored images. |
| \c pdg.animationDraw_AfterAll | 1 | After all authored images. This is the default. |
| \c pdg.animationDraw_BeforeSlot | 2 | Immediately before the named image. |
| \c pdg.animationDraw_AfterSlot | 3 | Immediately after the named image. |
| \c pdg.animationDraw_ReplaceSlot | 4 | In place of the named image. Other images remain visible. |

\par Stroke thickness
- \c pdg.animationStroke_PortPixels (0, default) keeps the Attributes line
  thickness in port pixels as the artwork scales.
- \c pdg.animationStroke_Local (1) scales line thickness with the artwork,
  including element transforms, nested drawings, and layer zoom. For unequal
  horizontal and vertical scaling, the thickness multiplier is
  \c sqrt(abs(det(composedTransform))), the geometric mean of the scale factors.
  The stroke keeps a uniform thickness rather than stretching its outline
  differently along each axis.

\par Bounds and visibility
Bounds allow PDG to skip drawing artwork that is outside the visible area.
When supplied, they must satisfy \c left <= \c right and \c top <= \c bottom.
Omit bounds, or set \c uncullable to \c true, when you cannot provide a rectangle
that safely contains all possible artwork.

\warning \ref Drawing::getBounds() "Drawing.getBounds()" reports geometry bounds, which do not include
complete stroke extents. Editing a drawing does not update the registration's
declared bounds. Include enough room for strokes and later edits, or leave the
registration uncullable. Visual bounds do not create physics or hit-test shapes.

\htmlonly[block]
</div>
\endhtmlonly

\par Editing and coordinates
PDG retains the drawing's shared contents. Changes made through the original
Drawing or its ElementRef objects appear on subsequent draws. This overload
does not invoke a JavaScript callback each frame.

Use Attributes to style drawing elements. Element transforms, the final bone
and sprite transforms, and the layer's conversion to port coordinates are
applied automatically. Opacity multiplies through the hierarchy. Do not
transform the artwork to world or port coordinates yourself.

Nested drawings retain their child contents and map the child's bounds to the
destination rectangle. ElementRef objects also keep drawing contents alive.
Drawing containment cycles are rejected.

\par Errors and registration changes
Registration throws if the pose is not enabled, the bone or slot is invalid,
or an option has an invalid type or value. A slot is not allowed with
\c pdg.animationDraw_BeforeAll or \c pdg.animationDraw_AfterAll.

Adding, removing, or enabling registrations during drawing takes effect after
the current frame. A rendering failure disables the failing registration;
getAnimationDrawableError() returns its diagnostic. Register the artwork again
to retry. A failed replacement leaves the authored image visible; a successful
replacement suppresses that image even when the Drawing is empty.

Returned drawings and their images remain alive until their rendering finishes.
Image opacity is restored after drawing, including on failure. Edits to shared
drawing contents and pixels already drawn are not rolled back by a later error.

\note Headless builds retain registrations but do not draw artwork or invoke
drawing callbacks. Use isAnimationDrawingSupported() to check drawing capability.

\sa Sprite::addAnimationDrawable(function, object)
\sa Sprite::enableAnimationPose()
\sa Sprite::setAnimationDrawableEnabled()
\sa Sprite::removeAnimationDrawable()
\sa Sprite::getAnimationDrawableError()

**/ }
